Give the Wayland backend a wl_shm window framebuffer.

WHY: upstream's Wayland video driver puts pixels on a surface only
through EGL; its one use of wl_shm is the mouse cursor
(SDL_waylandmouse.c). With SDL_OPENGL and SDL_OPENGLES off there is no
accelerated renderer, so SDL_video.c's texture path fails and it falls
back to the driver's native CreateWindowFramebuffer -- which Wayland
does not provide. The result was MEASURED twice (bare metal 2026-08-25,
and a minimal compositor built for the purpose, run 88916268912's
bundle): the window is created, configured and acked, the size follows
the guest's mode changes, 38 events flow in 12 seconds, and no buffer is
ever attached. A perfect handshake and never a pixel.

WHAT: the three native framebuffer entry points, on the driver's own
Wayland_SHMBuffer helper. Two shm buffers alternate so the one the
compositor holds is never the one being written; the caller draws into
a plain buffer that is copied into the next shm buffer on update, then
attach + damage (the driver's own version-4 idiom) + commit + flush.
Reached only when no accelerated renderer exists, so with GL present
nothing changes.

Read from the pinned tarball (sha256 5f5993c5...), three hunks,
generated with diff -u, applied with the same `patch -p1 -N` the driver
uses. Not compiled where it was written (no Wayland headers there): the
recipe's build is its first compile, and the tools bundle it produces,
against a compositor that dumps frames, is its first measurement.

